caption: Duration and Clearance
technology-rule: duration-and-clearance

A chem remains in the user’s system after its benefits end.

The Clearance Interval is:

Clearance Interval = Effect Duration * Clearance Multiplier

At R_Cl 0, the multiplier is 300. The following examples use the default R_Cl 0 formula:

A dose occupies Chem Capacity from activation until its residual Clearance Interval ends. Total occupancy time is Effect Duration + Clearance Interval.

| Effect duration | Clearance interval |
| --- | --- |
| 1 second | 5 minutes |
| 2 seconds | 10 minutes |
| 6 seconds | 30 minutes |
| 20 seconds | 100 minutes |
| 1 minute | 5 hours |
| 3 minutes | 15 hours |
| 10 minutes | 50 hours |
| 30 minutes | 150 hours |

Each dose clears independently.

Each formula must state its Redosing behavior. The normal profile is Refresh: a new dose resets the remaining benefit duration without increasing the effect’s level. Other permitted profiles are Extend, which adds duration; Stack, which adds effect levels up to a stated cap; Independent, in which each dose maintains its own active effect; No Additional Benefit; or a formula-specific Special rule.

Regardless of how the benefits interact, every physical dose has its own addiction roll, Clearance Interval, and Chem Capacity occupancy unless the formula explicitly establishes an integrated multi-dose mechanism. A Stack formula must state its maximum stack.
